<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carnuntum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carnuntum was a Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress and town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>. Capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> Superior, it lay on the Amber Road and was a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> was proclaimed emperor there in 193, and an imperial conference met there in 308.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carnuntum.jpg" alt="Carnuntum" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archaeotux at German Wikipedia (Original text: archaeotux) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carnuntum_map_Roman_city.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Carnuntum stood on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, near the villages of Petronell-Carnuntum and Bad Deutsch-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>. It was a Roman legionary fortress and the headquarters of the Pannonian fleet from about 50 CE, and it grew into a city of roughly 50,000 inhabitants. It was a major trading centre for amber, because the main arm of the Amber Road crossed the Danube there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Carnuntum first appears in history in 6 CE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> made it his base in the campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a>. It became the garrison of the Legio XV Apollinaris before 14 CE, and in about 50 CE the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> ordered a legion to be stationed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to protect the defeated party in a dispute between the Quadi and the Marcomanni. Legio XIV Gemina made it its permanent quarters in 117–118 and stayed for three centuries. Hadrian made it a municipium, and it became the capital of Pannonia Superior.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> lived there for three years, 172 to 175, during the war against the Marcomanni, and wrote part of his Meditations there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>, then governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, was proclaimed emperor by his soldiers there in 193. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severan-dynasty">Severan dynasty</a> the city boomed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> raised it to colony status. In 308 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> chaired the Conference of Carnuntum with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> to settle tensions within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a>. An earthquake damaged it around 350, and in 374 Germanic invaders, the Quadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a>, destroyed it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> partly restored it, but it never regained its importance, and Vindobona became the chief military centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site was eventually abandoned, used as a cemetery and a source of building stone, and its remains became buried and forgotten. The Carnuntum Archaeological Park now covers 10 square kilometres. It includes a rebuilt Roman house, palace ruins, an amphitheatre for about 15,000 spectators, and the Heidentor, the ruin of a triumphal arch put up between 354 and 361, probably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a>. A gladiator school was found in 2011 by aerial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a> and ground-penetrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carnuntum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 805 a Christian khagan, Theodore, asked Charlemagne for land between Savaria and <b>Carnuntum</b> because of attacks by Slavs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Marcus Aurelius made his headquarters at <b>Carnuntum</b>, and in 179 Marcus Valerius Maximianus won a decisive battle at Laugaricio (Trenčín).</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Roger Beck lists the places of the earliest phase (c. 80–120 CE): Rome, Nida and Mogontiacum in Germania, Pons Aeni in Noricum, <b>Carnuntum</b> in Pannonia, Novae and Oescus in Moesia, and Caesarea Maritima in Judaea.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest military inscription to Mithras was set up by a centurion of the Fifteenth Legion at <b>Carnuntum</b>, probably before 114, and in the 2nd and 3rd centuries the cult spread among soldiers, merchants and officials at an “astonishing” rate.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>After the murder of Pertinax, the legions at <b>Carnuntum</b> hailed their governor Septimius Severus as emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom grew wealthy on commerce along the Amber Road between the Baltic and <b>Carnuntum</b>, and Roman goods reached its elite graves.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vannius">Kingdom of Vannius</a></li></ul></section>
